{
  "dom_id": "step-q84752",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\nI. The number of families without children among $20$ families interviewed\n\nII. The number of players who score a goal in a soccer game among a total of $10$ players if each player has the same probability of scoring a goal.\n\nIII. The number of spectators in a soccer stadium",
  "worked_solution": "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of families without children among $20$ families interviewed can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $20$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of players who score a goal among $10$ players can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of spectators cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
